<task id="116" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>The status-drift check compares POMLs and TASK-INDEX rows as SETS — an unpaired entry on EITHER side is drift (ISS-025 / #1004)</title>
    <phase>Register-driven — queued 2026-09-18 (wave 0, bookkeeping)</phase>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>STANDARD</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>One repo tooling script; no product code, no new surface, no ADR question. Verification is behavioural — recreate the condition, then perturb the new assertion — not a code-review judgment call.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Fully specified change to one 230-line PowerShell script with a closed acceptance set. The one judgment boundary (telling status rows apart from the index's reference tables) is carried as an escalation trigger rather than left to the model.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Edits scripts/check-task-status-drift.ps1, which task-execute Step 10 and push-to-github Step 1.65 both GATE on. Changing it while another agent is completing a task would move that agent's gate under its feet.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>none</deps>
    <tags>tooling, scripts, bookkeeping, governance</tags>
    <estimated-effort>1–2 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>ISS-025 / #1004, owner-approved for queuing 2026-09-18 as task 116 (115 reserved for the §2 accuracy-repair pass). Found 2026-09-18 by creating the condition while queuing 109–114: six index rows with no POML, and the check printed both counts and still said "No drift".</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>scripts/check-task-status-drift.ps1 reconciles by iterating the POMLs and looking up each one's index marker, so it only ever examines tasks that exist on BOTH sides. A POML with no index row, and an index row with no POML, are both invisible to it. Make the check compare the two as SETS — equal in both directions — and report every unpaired entry as drift.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE tooling developer — PowerShell, the repo's task-bookkeeping scripts, and the two skills that gate on them.</role>
  <goal>The check answers "do the POMLs and the index describe the SAME set of tasks, and agree on each one's state?" instead of only the second half. An index row advertising work that no task file backs goes red at the same moment a stale status does, with the same message shape, and both gating callers keep working unchanged.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Register entry: notes/defer-issues.md § ISS-025 (GitHub #1004). Sequencing: notes/remediation-and-sequencing-plan-2026-09-18.md § "3. Workstream C" → "A gap in my own session-15 work", wave 0.

WHAT THE SCRIPT DOES TODAY. Invoke-ProjectCheck builds two hashtables — Get-PomlStatuses (id → &lt;status&gt; text) and Get-IndexMarkers (id → row marker) — then reconciles with:

    foreach ($id in ($poml.Keys | Sort-Object)) {
        if (-not $idx.ContainsKey($id)) { continue }   # no row to compare against

That `continue` is the whole defect. The loop is keyed on the POML side, so an index row with no POML is never visited; and the guarded POML-with-no-row case is SKIPPED rather than reported. Both counts are already printed ("task POMLs parsed" / "index rows parsed"), so the instrument has the evidence on screen and discards it.

DEMONSTRATED, NOT THEORISED (2026-09-18). After six 🔲 [open] rows for 109–114 were added with no task files, the script printed `task POMLs parsed : 105`, `index rows parsed : 111`, then "No drift: every task POML status agrees with its index marker.", rc=0. The verdict was not wrong, it was NARROW — true about what it measured and silent about what had changed.

THIS IS THE MIRROR OF A GUARD IT ALREADY HAS. The `$unparseable` guard (POMLs present but ZERO rows parsed) exists precisely so a parser that reads nothing cannot launder itself into a green check — FAILURE-MODES AP-12. An orphan row is the same class of blind spot one step in.

STATE OF THIS PROJECT (re-verified 2026-09-19, do not trust — re-measure): 111 POMLs, 111 index rows, zero unpaired entries in BOTH directions, rc=0. So the new check MUST still exit 0 here. If your reading says otherwise, stop and report it.

⚠️ A FINDING THE REGISTER DOES NOT CARRY — verify it before writing the set comparison. The current row regex matches 119 LINES but yields only 111 DISTINCT ids: eight ids match twice, and `$map[$id] = ...` keeps the LAST line. The second hit in each pair is not a status row — it comes from the "Dependencies added 2026-09-15" table (`| 055, 064 | 105 | ... |`, matching as id=064 marker='055,') and from the accuracy-audit table (`| **107** | ... |`, matching as id=107 marker='**'). Pairs: 036 (L591/L779), 064 (L628/L761), 082 (L186/L777), 088 (L651/L778), 093 (L194/L776), 094 (L195/L781), 095 (L196/L780), 107 (L715/L775). Those eight are all currently open on both sides, and a marker of '**' carries no done token and no done emoji, so it evaluates as open and nothing goes red TODAY — by luck, not by construction. Two consequences: (a) the set equality that holds today is real, but it is computed by a parser that also matches eight prose rows; (b) the day one of those eight completes, the reference row overrides its real ✅ marker and the EXISTING check reports phantom drift. A set comparison layered on this parser without deciding what a status row is will report a false orphan the first time the audit table names a task with no POML.

ROW SHAPE. Status rows live in the phase tables and look like `| 🔲 [open] 109 | ...description... | FR | Deps | Group | Safe | Tier | Effort |`, with the marker, the optional bracketed ASCII token and the id all in the SAME first cell (bold variants `**020**` occur). The index also holds a dependency table, an execution-sequence table (`| Wave | Order | Notes |`) and the accuracy-audit table — none of which are status rows.

🚨 MATCH THE ASCII TOKEN, NEVER THE EMOJI. grep / Select-String SILENTLY return 0 for characters above U+FFFF: 🔲 is U+1F532 (4 bytes) and matches nothing, while ✅ is U+2705 (3 bytes) and matches fine. That asymmetry is why `[open]`, `[done]`, `[wip]`, `[escalated]`, `[blocked]` exist in the index at all. FAILURE-MODES G-16. PowerShell itself handles non-BMP correctly, so the script's emoji fallback is for ~150 legacy indexes — but no NEW matcher may depend on a glyph above U+FFFF.

CALLERS. task-execute Step 10 ("VERIFY both writes landed", exit 1 = do not proceed) and push-to-github Step 1.65 (exit 0 → continue, exit 1 → stop) both invoke it with no arguments and read only the exit code. `-All` is the repo-wide observation mode and ALWAYS exits 0 by design: repo-wide drift is dominated by archived projects, and gating on that total would make the check red on day one and waived by day two.</background>
